WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.390
GPU rental is useful when flexibility matters more than ownership: temporary workloads,

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:10.140
model evaluations, bursts, distributed team access, and specialized memory requirements.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.820
Choose among major providers, specialized clouds, marketplaces, notebooks,

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.505
dedicated instances, and managed inference according to the control, support,

00:00:17.505 --> 00:00:20.270
interruption tolerance, and data boundary the job requires.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:21.796
Match the device to the workload.

00:00:21.796 --> 00:00:26.830
VRAM, memory bandwidth, GPU generation, software support, context, batch, concurrency,

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.215
and multimodal inputs all influence fit.

00:00:29.215 --> 00:00:32.150
Several GPUs do not automatically behave as one.

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:36.070
Test the smallest plausible configuration with real work and scale only when measurements

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:36.880
identify a limit.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.185
Control the complete cost.

00:00:38.185 --> 00:00:42.595
Include provisioning, idle time, persistent storage, caches, snapshots, data transfer,

00:00:42.595 --> 00:00:43.260
and cleanup.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.490
Use commitments for predictable demand and interruptible capacity for restartable work.

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:50.680
Budgets, alerts, ownership tags, automatic expiration,

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.655
and shutdown automation turn good intentions into controls.

00:00:53.655 --> 00:00:58.620
Build a reproducible environment, protect SSH and API access, keep secrets out of images,

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.160
and classify prompts and documents before upload.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.250
Monitor service health and hardware use, checkpoint important work,

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:06.155
and preserve a rebuild and rollback path.

00:01:06.155 --> 00:01:10.120
When the job is complete, stop or delete compute and account for every remaining disk,

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.140
snapshot, log, key, and network rule.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:16.330
Practical compute on demand means the rental is productive while active and leaves neither

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:19.600
uncontrolled spending nor exposed data after it ends.
